---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Plate from "../components/Plate.astro";
import "../styles/drawing.css";
import { computeHouse, TYPOLOGIES, TYPOLOGY_ORDER } from "../lib/house.js";
import { renderProfile } from "../lib/draw.js";

const houses = TYPOLOGY_ORDER.map((k) => {
  const t = TYPOLOGIES[k];
  const h = computeHouse({
    typology: k,
    widthKen: t.ken.w[2],
    depthKen: t.ken.d[2],
    exposure: k === "gyoka" ? "coastal" : k === "gassho" ? "snow" : "inland",
  });
  return { k, t, h, profile: renderProfile(h) };
});
---

<Layout
  title="Ibushi 燻 — akiya brought back to life | Hida, Gifu"
  description="A workshop in Hida that takes on abandoned minka — akiya — and brings them back into use on the old methods. Japan has nine million empty houses. We are asked about forty a year and we take four. A minka is cured by the smoke of being lived in."
  nav=""
>
  <!-- ================= hero ================= -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-media">
      {/* The still is the real content — srcset, alt, eager. The film layers
          over it and its first frame IS this still, so the fade-in is
          invisible. It plays ONCE and holds on the lit frame: looping back to
          a cold ridge would argue the opposite of the page. */}
      <div class="hero-film" data-hero-film>
        <img
          class="hero-film-still"
          src="/images/hero-akiya.jpg"
          srcset="/images/hero-akiya-800.jpg 800w, /images/hero-akiya.jpg 1536w"
          sizes="100vw"
          width="1536"
          height="1024"
          alt="A thatched farmhouse standing empty in a snowbound mountain valley on a clear winter morning. No smoke at the ridge, storm shutters closed and one hanging loose, grass rooted in the slumped thatch, and snow lying unbroken to the door — but the frame still square and sound."
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
        <video
          class="hero-film-v"
          muted
          playsinline
          preload="none"
          aria-hidden="true"
          tabindex="-1"
        >
          <source src="/video/first-smoke.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
        </video>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-plate-note spec gloss">
        Empty since 2011, and sound to the frame. This is the morning the fire
        was lit again.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap hero-text">
      <h1>A minka is cured<br />by the smoke of<br />being lived in.</h1>
      <div class="hero-cols">
        <p>
          There is no flue. The fire in the floor sends its smoke up through the
          open frame, out through the thatch and the vent at the ridge, and on
          the way it lays soot on every beam and every stem of straw it passes.
          That soot kills the insects, drives out the damp, and stops the rot.
        </p>
        <p class="spec gloss">
          Which means the building is preserved by being inhabited — so a house
          whose fire goes out is not sad, it is failing. Japan now has nine
          million empty houses. We take the ones whose frames are still worth
          the work, and we ask every owner the same question before anything
          else: <em>will the fire be lit?</em>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="snap" />

  <!-- ================= akiya ================= -->
  <section class="akiya">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">Akiya · 空き家</span>
      <h2 class="akiya-h">
        Nine million houses stand empty.<br />Most of them should come down.
      </h2>

      <div class="akiya-grid">
        <div class="akiya-text">
          <p>
            An <span class="term">akiya</span> 空き家 is a house nobody lives in.
            The 2023 Housing and Land Survey counted nine million of them —
            13.8% of every dwelling in the country, double the number in 1993.
            Around six in ten were inherited. In six in ten, the house went
            quiet when its owner died.
          </p>
          <p>
            We are not in that business, and we want to be exact about why.
            Most akiya are post-war frame or prefab, built after 1980, with
            nothing in them worth a carpenter's year. They have no
            <span class="term">daikokubashira</span> 大黒柱 worth keeping, no
            curved <span class="term">magari-bari</span> 曲がり梁 overhead, and
            no roof that would take thatch again. The honest answer for those
            houses is a demolition contractor, and we will tell an owner so in
            the first hour.
          </p>
          <p>
            The ones we take are <span class="term">kominka</span> 古民家 — the
            old houses, pre-war and mostly pre-Meiji, built by hand out of one
            valley's timber. Those are disappearing faster than the empty
            flats. Between 2008 and 2013 the count of kominka fell
            <strong>13%</strong>. The Japan Kominka Association puts what is
            left at around 1,250,000. Every one taken down is a frame that
            cannot be built again at any price, because the trees are gone and
            so, nearly, are the men who cut the joints.
          </p>
          <p class="spec gloss">
            So we are selective, and not out of taste. A frame either carries
            another two hundred years or it does not, and saying yes to a house
            that cannot is how a workshop spends four years proving a point to
            a building that was always going to lose.
          </p>
          <a class="akiya-link" href="/mitate">
            The standard we apply, in full <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>

        <div class="akiya-media">
          <Plate
            name="declined"
            alt="A thatched farmhouse past saving on a clear winter morning: the ridge broken, the roof folded inward along its length, thatch fallen into the interior, one gable wall leaning out and a corner post off its stone."
            ratio="3 / 2"
            caption="Declined. The ridge has gone and taken the tie-beams with it; the frame is no longer carrying itself. Photographed for the record, not for the pity of it."
          />
          <dl class="akiya-figs">
            <div><dt>Vacant dwellings, 2023</dt><dd>9,000,000 · 13.8%</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Kominka, 2008–2013</dt><dd>−13%</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Kominka thought to remain</dt><dd>~1,250,000</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Asked of us each year</dt><dd>About forty</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Taken</dt><dd>Four</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="akiya-after">
      <Plate
        name="hero-smoke"
        alt="A thatched farmhouse at first light across a frosted field, one straight column of woodsmoke standing off the ridge into cold blue air, mountains behind."
        ratio="16 / 9"
      />
      <figcaption class="wrap">
        <span class="kicker">The other end of the work · 再び</span>
        <p>
          A house we took, the second winter after. Smoke at the ridge before
          seven in the morning — which is the only proof any of this works, and
          the only one we accept.
        </p>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <hr class="snap" />

  <!-- ================= the argument ================= -->
  <section class="argument">
    <div class="wrap arg-grid">
      <div class="arg-media">
        <Plate
          name="susu-beam"
          alt="Extreme close detail of two hundred years of hearth soot lacquered onto a massive zelkova beam, the adze-cut scallops still legible beneath, raking daylight across the surface."
          ratio="4 / 5"
          caption="Two hundred years of soot on a keyaki tie-beam. The adze marks are still legible under it."
        />
      </div>
      <div class="arg-text">
        <span class="kicker">Why the smoke matters · 煤</span>
        <h2>Soot is a finish, not a stain.</h2>
        <p>
          A beam over a working irori takes on a hard, near-black lacquer that
          you cannot buy and cannot fake. It takes decades. Insects will not
          bore it, water will not sit on it, and the fungi that destroy
          untreated timber cannot get a hold.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same smoke rises into the roof and hardens the thatch from
          beneath, which is why a smoked kayabuki roof lasts three or four
          times as long as one over a house that stands empty. It is also why
          the bamboo taken out of an old roof at re-thatch — <span class="term"
            >susutake</span
          > — is worth more than the bamboo that went in.
        </p>
        <p class="spec gloss">
          The trade-off is real and we will not pretend otherwise: an open
          hearth means smoke in the room, and a house run this way is warmer,
          darker at the ceiling, and needs its fire kept. Clients who want the
          look without the fire get a building that will need us again in
          thirty years.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="snap" />

  <!-- ================= the four houses ================= -->
  <section class="four">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">The four houses · 四つの民家</span>
      <h2>One valley, four answers.</h2>
      <p class="four-lede">
        <span class="term">Minka</span> 民家 means, plainly, "the houses of the
        people" — everyone who was not a samurai or a priest. It is not one
        building type. It is four, and the differences between them are not
        style: they are answers to four different climates and four different
        ways of earning a living. From this workshop all four are within a
        morning's drive.
      </p>

      <ul class="four-list">
        {
          houses.map(({ k, t, h, profile }) => (
            <li class="four-item">
              <a href={`/houses/${k}`}>
                <div class="four-profile" set:html={profile} />
                <h3>
                  {t.label} <span class="jp four-jp">{t.jp}</span>
                </h3>
                <p class="four-gloss spec">{t.gloss}</p>
                <p class="four-line">{t.line}</p>
                <dl class="four-spec">
                  <div>
                    <dt>Kōbai</dt>
                    <dd>
                      {Math.round(h.kobai * 10) / 10} sun · {Math.round(h.deg)}°
                    </dd>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <dt>Roof</dt>
                    <dd>{h.thatch > 0 ? "Kaya thatch" : "Kawara tile"}</dd>
                  </div>
                </dl>
                <span class="four-go spec">Read the house →</span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="snap" />

  <!-- ================= the signature ================= -->
  <section class="sig">
    <div class="wrap sig-grid">
      <div class="sig-text">
        <span class="kicker">Sumitsuke · 墨付け</span>
        <h2>Nothing is cut until the line is drawn.</h2>
        <p>
          Before a joint is cut, the <span class="term">tōryō</span> walks the
          stacked timber with an ink pot and a chalked cord and snaps a
          dead-straight line onto every crooked log. That marking is called <span class="term">sumitsuke</span>, and it decides the house.
        </p>
        <p>
          Snap yours. Choose the house, the bays in <span class="term">ken</span
          >, the weather the site actually gets, and how much of the ground stays
          earth — and the section and plan redraw from those numbers alone, at a
          real drafting scale that changes as the house does. The specification
          it produces is what we would build from.
        </p>
        <a class="btn" href="/sumitsuke">Snap a line</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sig-media">
        <Plate
          name="toryo"
          alt="A master carpenter in his sixties crouched over a squared timber, snapping a straight ink line onto the wood with a traditional ink pot and cord, his face lit by daylight from a large open doorway."
          ratio="4 / 5"
          caption="Ōno Sōhachi marking a tie-beam. Sixth of his line to hold the title of tōryō."
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="snap" />

  <!-- ================= the frame + the record ================= -->
  <section class="ends">
    <div class="wrap ends-grid">
      <article class="end">
        <Plate
          name="magari-bari"
          alt="Looking up into a roof frame: enormous naturally curved pine beams left in the shape the tree grew, crossing overhead, joined without nails and blackened by hearth smoke."
          ratio="3 / 2"
        />
        <h3>The frame</h3>
        <p>
          Posts standing loose on stones so the house can ride an earthquake.
          Beams left in the curve the tree grew. Joints cut to hold without a
          single nail — because a nail rusts, and a joint does not.
        </p>
        <a class="quiet-link" href="/frame">Kigumi, ishiba-date, and the great pillar →</a>
      </article>

      <article class="end">
        <Plate
          name="tatami-shoji"
          alt="A tatami-matted room in the morning, paper screens glowing with low sun and casting their lattice across the mats, a timber-framed panel half open to a further room."
          ratio="3 / 2"
        />
        <h3>The rooms</h3>
        <p>
          Earth, then a step, then board, then matting. The house gets softer,
          quieter, and more formal the deeper you walk, and nobody has to be
          told where they may go.
        </p>
        <a class="quiet-link" href="/rooms">Doma to engawa, in order →</a>
      </article>

      <article class="end">
        <Plate
          name="yui-rethatch"
          alt="About twenty villagers on the steep thatched roof of a farmhouse, passing up bundles of golden miscanthus and combing the new thatch flush, old grey thatch stripped from one half."
          ratio="3 / 2"
        />
        <h3>The record</h3>
        <p>
          Every house we have raised, moved, or revived, entered as a <span class="term">munafuda</span> — the tag nailed inside the ridge
          naming who built it, for whom, and when.
        </p>
        <a class="quiet-link" href="/munafuda">The record since 1968 →</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="snap" />

  <section class="cta">
    <div class="wrap cta-in">
      <div>
        <h2>Will the fire be lit?</h2>
        <p>
          It is the first thing we ask. Tell us the site and the weather, and we
          will tell you plainly whether we are the right workshop for it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="btn" href="/commission">Begin a commission</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
  // The hero film. Only under .fx — ?nofx and prefers-reduced-motion both land
  // on .nofx, and there the still stands alone, which is a complete picture on
  // its own (the cold house is the point). Nothing is downloaded in that case
  // either: preload="none" and we never call load().
  (() => {
    const wrap = document.querySelector("[data-hero-film]");
    if (!wrap || !document.documentElement.classList.contains("fx")) return;
    const v = wrap.querySelector("video");
    if (!v || !v.canPlayType || !v.canPlayType("video/mp4")) return;

    let started = false;
    const start = () => {
      if (started) return;
      started = true;
      v.addEventListener("playing", () => v.classList.add("is-playing"), { once: true });
      // If autoplay is refused, the still simply stays — no broken state.
      const p = v.play();
      if (p && p.catch) p.catch(() => {});
    };

    if (!("IntersectionObserver" in window)) return start();
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        for (const e of entries) {
          if (e.isIntersecting) {
            io.disconnect();
            start();
          }
        }
      },
      { threshold: 0.25 }
    );
    io.observe(wrap);
  })();
</script>

<style>
  /* --- hero -------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .hero {
    padding-block: 0 clamp(3rem, 6vw, 6rem);
  }

  .hero-media :global(.plate img),
  .hero-media :global(.plate-hold) {
    max-height: 74vh;
  }

  .hero-film {
    position: relative;
    line-height: 0;
  }

  .hero-film-still {
    width: 100%;
    max-height: 74vh;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }

  .hero-film-v {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 0.6s linear;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* frame one of the film is the still, so this reads as nothing happening
     until the smoke starts */
  .hero-film-v.is-playing {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .hero-text {
    padding-top: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
  }

  .hero h1 {
    margin-bottom: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.25rem);
  }

  .hero-cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(19rem, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 4rem);
    max-width: 62rem;
  }

  /* --- argument ---------------------------------------------------------- */

  .argument {
    padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 7vw, 7rem);
  }

  /* --- akiya -------------------------------------------------------------
     The hero is now a cold house, so this section carries the argument for
     why it is cold and what we do about it. It ends on hero-smoke — the same
     kind of building, working — so the page opens on the problem and closes
     the section on the proof. */

  .hero-plate-note {
    max-width: 34rem;
    margin: 0.7rem auto 0;
    padding-inline: var(--gut);
  }

  .akiya {
    padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem) 0;
  }

  .akiya-h {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 3rem);
  }

  .akiya-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 0.9fr);
    gap: clamp(1.75rem, 5vw, 5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .akiya-text p + p {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .akiya-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--okibi);
    padding-bottom: 0.15rem;
  }

  /* the figures sit under the declined plate as a working table, not a
     stat-card row — this is a record, not a pitch deck */
  .akiya-figs {
    margin-top: 1.6rem;
    border-top: var(--rule);
  }

  .akiya-figs > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 0.55rem 0;
    border-bottom: var(--hair);
  }

  .akiya-figs dt {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--sugi-deep);
  }

  .akiya-figs dd {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    text-align: right;
  }

  .akiya-after {
    margin: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem) 0 0;
  }

  .akiya-after figcaption {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .akiya-after figcaption p {
    max-width: 40rem;
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }

  .arg-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.85fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(1.75rem, 5vw, 5rem);
